::CW_U03_L04_Q01::[MLA.CW.WR.01] Central idea: Avoiding responsibility can make guilt grow. Which detail best supports it?{
~A. The sky is blue.#Incorrect. This does not connect to responsibility. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. The character eats cereal.#Incorrect. This is unrelated unless tied to guilt.
~C. The hallway is long.#Incorrect. This does not support the idea by itself.
=D. The character hides the broken key after blaming someone else.#Correct. Hiding evidence after blame shows guilt growing.
}

::CW_U03_L04_Q02::[MLA.CW.WR.01] Passage: Mina says she does not need help, then drops three boxes and finally lets her sister carry one. What central idea is best supported?{
~A. Boxes are always empty.#Incorrect. The boxes are difficult to carry. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Accepting help can be a form of strength.#Correct. Mina changes from refusal to shared effort.
~C. Sisters never help.#Incorrect. The sister helps. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Dropping things is the only idea.#Incorrect. The drop supports a larger idea. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L04_Q03::[MLA.CW.WR.01] Central idea: Memory can make a new place feel less lonely. Which detail is most relevant?{
~A. The desk has four screws.#Incorrect. This is unrelated unless connected to memory.
~B. The window is square.#Incorrect. Shape alone does not support the idea.
=C. The narrator hangs a photo from home above the new desk.#Correct. The photo connects memory to the new place.
~D. The floor is flat.#Incorrect. This does not develop memory or loneliness.
}

::CW_U03_L04_Q04::[MLA.CW.WR.01] A draft includes a long description of an unrelated concert poster. The central idea is about avoiding an apology. What revision decision is best?{
=A. Cut the poster or revise it so it connects to the apology conflict.#Correct. Details should support the central idea. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. Keep it because all details are equally useful.#Incorrect. Irrelevant detail can distract. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Add more unrelated posters.#Incorrect. This increases distraction. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Remove the apology instead.#Incorrect. The apology is central. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L04_Q05::[MLA.CW.WR.01] Central idea: True confidence includes admitting what you do not know. Which detail supports it?{
~A. The character pretends to understand and leaves.#Incorrect. That shows avoidance. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. The character asks for help with the first step in front of the group.#Correct. The action shows honest confidence. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. The character describes a pencil.#Incorrect. Unconnected description does not support the idea.
~D. The character refuses to speak forever.#Incorrect. That does not show confidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L04_Q06::[MLA.CW.WR.01] What is a relevant detail?{
~A. Any detail that is long.#Incorrect. Length does not make relevance. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. A detail that distracts from the idea.#Incorrect. That is irrelevant. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. A detail with no connection.#Incorrect. Connection is required. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. A detail that helps develop the central idea.#Correct. Relevance depends on connection to the idea.
}

::CW_U03_L04_Q07::[MLA.CW.WR.01] Passage: Jori keeps checking the empty chair after her friend moves away. Central idea: Absence can shape how a place feels. How does the detail support the idea?{
~A. Chairs cannot matter.#Incorrect. Objects can support ideas. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. The detail is only about furniture shopping.#Incorrect. The context is absence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. The empty chair makes absence visible in the setting.#Correct. The chair turns missing someone into a concrete detail.
~D. Moving away is unrelated.#Incorrect. It explains the empty chair's meaning.
}

::CW_U03_L04_Q08::[MLA.CW.WR.01] Central idea: Small acts of care can rebuild trust. Which detail best supports it?{
=A. The character leaves a repaired notebook on the friend's desk without demanding forgiveness.#Correct. The action shows care and patience. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. The character ignores the friend forever.#Incorrect. That does not rebuild trust. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. The character changes socks.#Incorrect. This is unrelated. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. The character lists every desk in the room.#Incorrect. Listing desks does not support trust. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L04_Q09::[MLA.CW.WR.01] Which central idea is strongest for fiction development?{
~A. The scene is about stuff.#Incorrect. This is vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Keeping secrets can protect someone briefly but damage trust over time.#Correct. This is specific and can guide details.
~C. A person does things.#Incorrect. This gives no direction. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. There is a room.#Incorrect. This is only setting. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L04_Q10::[MLA.CW.WR.01] Central idea: Hope can survive failure. Which detail supports it?{
~A. The farmer burns every seed.#Incorrect. That works against hope. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. The field has dirt.#Incorrect. This is too general. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. After the failed harvest, the farmer saves seeds for spring.#Correct. Saving seeds prepares for another chance. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. The calendar has dates.#Incorrect. Not enough connection. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L04_Q11::[MLA.CW.WR.01] Passage: Ana keeps rereading the invitation but never opens the door. Central idea: Fear can keep people from accepting connection. What detail supports it?{
~A. The door is made of wood.#Incorrect. Material alone does not support the idea.
~B. The invitation has ink.#Incorrect. This detail is too general. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Ana knows the alphabet.#Incorrect. Not relevant. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Ana rereads the invitation but does not open the door.#Correct. The action shows desire and hesitation. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L04_Q12::[MLA.CW.WR.01] What should a writer do with an interesting detail that does not support the central idea?{
~A. Keep it no matter what.#Incorrect. Interesting can still be distracting. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Revise it so it supports the idea, or cut it.#Correct. The detail needs a purpose. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. Add ten more unrelated details.#Incorrect. More distraction weakens focus. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Delete the central idea.#Incorrect. The central idea guides the scene. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L04_Q13::[MLA.CW.WR.01] Central idea: Pride can prevent a person from learning. Which detail supports it?{
=A. The character closes the instruction book after one page and says, "I already know."#Correct. The action and dialogue reveal pride blocking learning.
~B. The character studies carefully.#Incorrect. That works against the idea. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. The character asks a mentor for advice.#Incorrect. That shows openness. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. The character admits confusion.#Incorrect. That shows humility. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L04_Q14::[MLA.CW.WR.01] Passage: The narrator carries a cracked bowl into the new apartment because it belonged to her mother. Central idea: Memory can make change bearable. How does the bowl support it?{
~A. The bowl proves nothing changes.#Incorrect. The new apartment shows change. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. The bowl is unrelated because it is cracked.#Incorrect. The crack may make it more meaningful.
=C. The bowl carries memory into the new place.#Correct. It connects past relationship to present change.
~D. The bowl only shows cooking instructions.#Incorrect. No recipe is mentioned. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L04_Q15::[MLA.CW.WR.01] Which detail distracts from the central idea "honesty can repair harm"?{
~A. The character admits the lie.#Incorrect. This supports the idea. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
