}

::CW_U03_L04_Q24::[MLA.CW.WR.01] What is the best use of central idea during drafting?{
=A. Use it to choose details, conflicts, and character actions that support the same meaning.#Correct. Central idea guides development. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. Ignore it after writing one sentence.#Incorrect. It should guide the scene. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Use it only for the title.#Incorrect. It affects the whole scene. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Replace all details with one vague word.#Incorrect. Fiction needs concrete support. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L04_Q25::[MLA.CW.WR.01] A student writes, "This detail supports the idea because I like it." What feedback is best?{
~A. Personal preference is always enough evidence.#Incorrect. The detail must support the idea. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Explain how the detail develops the central idea, not just personal preference.#Correct. Support requires a clear connection to meaning.
~C. Remove the central idea from the answer.#Incorrect. The idea is necessary. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Add unrelated details until it feels longer.#Incorrect. Length does not create support. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}
